  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An upper ridge centered over the Southeast CONUS and \nsfc high pressure ridging in from the north will keep generally \ndry conditions across ENC through friday afternoon. One last\nmorning in the 60s before TD's surge more on Friday as a \nprefrontal trough and cold front approach as high pres cont to\nmove further offshore. Warm and humid will cont through at least\nearly next week as PW's rise back above climo.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The aforementioned cold front and trough\nexpected to move into and through ENC through Sat. Sfc \nconvergence and lowering heights aloft as shortwave energy rides\nover the upper ridge along with increasing moisture will allow \nfor a better chance of showers and perhaps a few storms late\ntoday, and esp this evening. Front slowly sags into srn \nportions of ENC Sat, with best chances for showers and iso \nthunderstorms for srn areas on Sat. Fcst a bit more muddled \nbeyond Sat as it will depend on the front location, but at this\ntime it appears typical seabreeze showers/storms Sunday into\nearly next week as return flow develops once again. \n\nKey Message 3...Next week, a strong high pressure system \nbecomes anchored acrs New England and ern Canada, while at the\nsame time low pres develops off the Mid Atlantic coast, \ntightening the pressure gradient along the mid- atlantic and \nnortheast. This will likely usher in stronger NE winds to the \nregion. Given it is 5-6 days out, there is still much\nuncertainty, with the 18/00Z GFS/ECMWF a bit weaker with this\nsolution, while the AIFS cont to indicate a stronger system. For\nnow there is good model consensus on at least higher waves \nalong the Outer Banks mid- next week. This will be worth keeping\nan eye on, as a long fetch of strong winds could bring higher \nwave energy towards vulnerable portions of the Outer Banks. It \nis too soon to determine specifics at this point and trends will\nhave to be monitored in the coming days.",
